<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jeonju</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wansanju</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jeonju is the capital of North Jeolla province in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>. Known as Wansanju in the Unified Silla period, it became the capital of Later Baekje when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeon-hwon">Gyeon Hwon</a> proclaimed himself king there in 900. As the ancestral seat of the Jeonju Yi clan, the royal family of Joseon, it holds a shrine with a portrait of King Taejo, and it is known for its traditional houses and cuisine.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeonju.jpg" alt="Jeonju" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jeonju_Hanok_Maeul_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jeonju lies in a basin surrounded by mountains, with Moryongsan to the north and Namgosan to the south, and the Jeonjucheon River flows through the centre of the city. Wanju County almost entirely surrounds it, so the city is both urban and rural. Its name is glossed as &quot;Perfect region&quot;. Jeonju is divided into two wards, Deokjin District and Wansan District.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The kingdom of Baekje lay in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and included the area of Jeonju, which is believed to have been founded as a market town within Baekje around 57 BCE. Silla conquered Baekje in 660, and in 685 Jeonju became one of the nine provincial capitals of the Silla kingdom. From 889 peasant revolts caused by over-taxation spread across the kingdom, and in Jeonju they gave a base to one of the most powerful rebel leaders, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeon-hwon">Gyeon Hwon</a>. He renamed the city Wansan and made it the capital of Later Baekje. From there he campaigned against Silla, destroying its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Geumseong</a> and assassinating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongae-of-silla">King Gyeongae</a> in 927. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-goryeo">Wang Geon</a> of Goryeo then invaded Later Baekje in 934, and Jeonju surrendered to him in 935.</p><p>Under Goryeo, Jeonju was again a provincial capital and enjoyed relative stability and economic growth. In 1182 peasant rebels took the city with the help of government troops stationed there, who resented being made to do heavy labour alongside slaves, but the rebellion was suppressed forty days after it began. The Joseon dynasty, founded in 1392, regarded Jeonju as its ancestral home. It became the capital of a reorganised Jeolla province, and in 1413 it was one of four cities given copies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-the-joseon-dynasty">Annals of the Joseon Dynasty</a> to keep. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donghak-peasant-revolution">Donghak Peasant Revolution</a> occupied the town in 1894.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> from 1910 to 1945 and renamed Jeonju Zenshū. The Japanese authorities destroyed the old city walls, leaving only the gate Pungnammun. Jeonju received metropolitan status in 1935 and its modern boundaries and government in 1963. It was not on the front line in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a>, but suffered bombardment and the loss of many male residents who fought.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Jeonju is the capital and largest city of Jeonbuk province in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, known for its cuisine, historic buildings and festivals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> named the city a Creative City for Gastronomy in 2012, and Jeonju bibimbap is a well-known local dish. Jeonju Hanok Village holds over 800 traditional hanok buildings. Gyeonggijeon enshrines the portrait of Lee Seonggye, the first king of Joseon, and the Jeongdong Catholic Church, built from 1908 to 1914, stands on the site of the Korean Catholic martyrs of 1791 and 1801.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jeonju” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The twelve requests accepted at the Treaty of <b>Jeonju</b> asked for punishment of corrupt officials and of yangban and seonbi, freedom for slaves and for the Cheonmin class, legal remarriage for widows, lower taxes, the picking of politicians by qualities rather than families, the cancelling of debts and…</blockquote><a href="/movement/donghak-peasant-revolution">Donghak Peasant Revolution · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>The general Yi Seong-gye founded the dynasty as King Taejo in 1392, and his descendants of the <b>Jeonju</b> Yi clan reigned until its end.</blockquote><a href="/societies/joseon">Social Hierarchy of Joseon Korea</a></li><li><blockquote>In 900 he proclaimed himself king at <b>Wansanju</b>, modern Jeonju, claiming to avenge the old kingdom of Baekje destroyed by Silla.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-baekje">Later Baekje</a></li></ul></section>
